      "He never got the chance.",
      "Within five minutes, Abdul’s natural charisma and hospitality instincts broke the stiff atmosphere into pieces. He talked about his hotel in Dubai; Naba’s father talked about local trade. They discovered mutual acquaintances. They debated import tariffs. Within twenty minutes, the two older men were laughing over steaming cups of mint tea like old schoolmates catching up at a reunion.",
      "Ma’un opened his mouth once: “If I may humbly state my sincere intentions regarding—”",
      "“Nonsense, Abdul!” Naba’s father boomed, completely overriding him with a wave of his hand. “The boy has a good face! A solid family! Why delay? The ninth of November. We do the nikah right here. Agreed?”",
      "“The ninth of November it is!” Abdul declared, shaking his hand firmly.",
      "Ma’un sat frozen, his speech still sitting in his throat like a swallowed coin. *Wait. Did my entire future just get decided in under twenty minutes over tea?*",
      "Then, from the small open window above the courtyard, the Asr adhan drifted into the room.",
      "Ma’un rose instantly, before the second phrase had finished. It was pure instinct. His fingers brushed against his black watch strap—a quiet, sacred reflex born from an afternoon he never spoke about.",
      "After they prayed together in the parlour, Naba’s father set down his prayer mat and smiled generously. “Any questions from the groom before we finalize?”",
      "Abdul looked at his son, expecting a polite *No, Uncle, everything is a blessing.*",
      "Ma’un hesitated. Then, with quiet sincerity, his voice carried across the quiet parlour: “Just one, sir. Is there anything she doesn’t like? Food, habits, anything at all. I would rather know now than make her uncomfortable by accident.”",
      "Through the thin partition wall, in the back room, a ceramic teacup stopped dead in mid-air.",
      "Naba was standing by the tray, her hands gripping the porcelain with knuckles turned pale. Beside her, her younger sister Sara sat perched on the low ottoman, watching her elder sister with wide, curious eyes.",
      "Naba knew that voice.",
      "She had heard it once, only a few mornings ago outside the pharmacy, offering to take all her sorries with the nervous wheeze of a deflating bicycle tyre.",
      "Her heart slammed against her ribs. *He’s real. He’s sitting right on the other side of that wall. And he’s asking about what I don’t like.*",
      "Then came her father’s voice through the plaster, hearty and dismissive: “Oh, she likes everything! She never makes a fuss. She’s no trouble at all.”",
